---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const tocItems = [
  { id: "entrepreneurship-portfolio", label: "AI Entrepreneurship Portfolio" },
  { id: "market-shaping", label: "Market-Shaping Discovery" },
  { id: "evaluations", label: "What to Keep and What to Cut" },
  { id: "assessment-platform", label: "Assessment Platform" },
  { id: "this-portfolio", label: "This portfolio" },
  { id: "ai-edtech-journal", label: "AI + EdTech Journal" },
];
---

<BaseLayout sideBarActiveItemID="projects" title="Projects | Agueda Schwartz" description="Four projects: a 0-to-1 AI product, market-shaping discovery, platform and vendor evaluations, and an assessment platform. Plus two personal tracks." maxWidthClass="lg:max-w-[1200px]">
  <div class="mt-5 lg:flex lg:gap-12">
    <aside class="hidden lg:block lg:w-52 lg:flex-shrink-0">
      <nav class="sticky top-8 text-sm">
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider opacity-60 mb-3 font-semibold">Projects</div>
        <ul class="space-y-1 border-l border-base-300">
          {tocItems.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={`#${item.id}`}
                class="toc-link block py-1.5 pl-4 -ml-px border-l-2 border-transparent opacity-60 hover:opacity-100 hover:border-base-content transition-all leading-snug font-medium"
              >
                {item.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
      <h1 class="sr-only">Projects</h1>
      <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider opacity-60 mb-3 lg:mb-8 lg:pb-4 lg:border-b lg:border-base-300">~5 min read · 4 projects and 2 personal tracks</div>

      <details class="lg:hidden mb-8 pb-4 border-b border-base-300 group">
        <summary class="practice-summary cursor-pointer flex items-center justify-between text-xs uppercase tracking-wider opacity-60 font-semibold py-2">
          <span>On this page</span>
          <span class="text-base opacity-60 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1 border-l border-base-300">
          {tocItems.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={`#${item.id}`}
                class="toc-link block py-1.5 pl-4 -ml-px border-l-2 border-transparent opacity-60 hover:opacity-100 hover:border-base-content transition-all leading-snug font-medium text-sm"
              >
                {item.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </details>

      <div class="prose max-w-none mb-12">
        <p>
          Four projects across a decade, from a 20-campus school network in Peru to a 0-to-1 AI product. The through-line is the same in all of them: find out whether the thing actually works, then turn that into a decision a team can act on. How I run the work day to day is on the <a href="/practice" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Practice page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: AI Entrepreneurship Portfolio -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="entrepreneurship-portfolio" class="!mt-0 text-2xl font-bold mb-1">AI Entrepreneurship Portfolio</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">WGU Labs · Product Manager · 2025 to 2026</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>A 0-to-1 AI product for early-stage founders. A five-person team had stalled on it for two months; when they were reassigned I was told to carry it with AI instead, with fractional support from a UX researcher and an engineer. Leadership wrapped it in June 2026.</p>

          <p><strong>Research that changed what got built.</strong> Six months of desk research had gone in and nobody had spoken to a user. I ran the interviews. Founders described the AI as a feedback-giver, not the collaborator the product had assumed. I checked that against behavior with a nine-day market test, and the community-led message converted at nearly twice the rate of the AI-led one. The product changed direction.</p>

          <p><strong>A standard the product could be judged against.</strong> I wrote the behavior specification: five guiding principles, seven interaction patterns and five hard boundaries on what the AI should not do inside a user's work, each grounded in interviews and peer-feedback documents instead of in anyone's preference.</p>

          <p><strong>Criteria written before the evidence.</strong> The market-test result was ambiguous and could have gone either way. I had written the go, pivot and kill criteria twelve days before the test went live, so the call came out as a decision instead of an argument.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="market-shaping" class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">Market-Shaping Discovery</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">WGU Labs · Product Manager · 2026</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>A method our sponsor designed for finding where a product could win before anyone builds it. I run the first stage.</p>

          <p><strong>Agents fan out, I make the calls they cannot.</strong> Scoped agents run evidence and competitive scans in parallel. What they cannot do is decide what the domain is, which opportunity is worth taking, or whether a candidate is strong enough to move. I set the frame and make those reads.</p>

          <p><strong>Testing the product and publishing what it could not do.</strong> Our scoring feature had never been tested against anything but its own documentation. I ran the same assessment twice, once with a strong submission and once with a deliberately weak one, then added an off-domain case and one with a planted omission. One scorer ranked the weak submission higher. I wrote it up with the limit of my own test stated in the same document: single runs are not a reliability study.</p>

          <p><strong>The product definition.</strong> Now at its fourth revision with the engineer and the sponsor, defining what is being built, what the first stage produces, and which of two user types carries the harder part of the work.</p>

          <p><strong>Worked examples, including one built to fail.</strong> The method needed something concrete to be demonstrated from. One example is a deliberate re-run with the product removed, to check whether a result held on its own or only because we already knew the answer we wanted.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="evaluations" class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">What to Keep and What to Cut</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">Innova Schools 2016 to 2017 · WGU Labs 2024 · the same work, eight years apart</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p><strong>Four learning platforms, no agreed way to judge them.</strong> They ran across a 20-campus network with real budget behind them. I designed the evaluations, tracking adoption, completion and frequency of use alongside learning outcomes, because usage is not comprehension. My recommendations were the evidence leadership used to decide what to scale and what to shut off.</p>

          <p><strong>Eighteen AI vendors, no definition of good.</strong> Our team had to choose vendors for a new institution. As one of three on the infrastructure team I wrote the needs assessment that turned the goals into a requirements set, then scored eighteen vendors against an eight-dimension rubric and delivered a shortlist the research teams could act on.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="assessment-platform" class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">Assessment Platform</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">Innova Schools · Product Owner · 2017 to 2018</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>Leadership asked for reporting dashboards. Talking to users showed the ask was not the need: the problem was upstream, in how data got entered under deadline. I cut the dashboard and shipped a data-entry tool that saved automatically and worked offline. Input errors fell 30% across 30,000 transactions and task time fell 10%.</p>

          <p><a href="/prd/assessment-platform/">Read the full product brief →</a></p>
        </div>
      </section>


      <div class="divider my-16"></div>

      <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] opacity-60 mb-6 font-semibold">Personal tracks</div>

      <!-- Section 2: This portfolio -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="this-portfolio" class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">This portfolio</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">Personal · Maintained 2024 to Present</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>
            I treat my portfolio as a project I run on the same operating model I use for product work. The point is the discipline of maintaining a public surface that reflects how I actually think, on a cadence I can sustain.
          </p>

          <p><strong>An evolving site</strong> built with Astro, TailwindCSS, and DaisyUI, deployed through GitHub Actions to a custom domain, with PostHog analytics for visibility into how readers find the work.</p>

          <p><strong>Custom components</strong> built for specific editorial purposes: a pull-quote treatment for thesis statements, a callout pattern for worked examples, a sticky table of contents with scroll-spy for long-form pages.</p>

          <p><strong>Notes</strong> that capture what I am learning at work, in sanitized form, tagged across four categories: methods, experiments, failures and signals. The archive lives at <a href="/notes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Notes</a>.</p>

          <p><strong>Written from the work itself</strong>, not as a separate exercise, which is the only reason they get written at all.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: AI + EdTech Journal -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 id="ai-edtech-journal" class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">AI + EdTech Journal</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-6">Personal · Started 2026</div>

        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>
            A personal intelligence journal for tracking AI and EdTech developments. The discipline is integrating what I read back into the work I am doing, so reading is not a separate activity from building.
          </p>

          <p><strong>A capture system that does not break flow.</strong> A Chrome extension I built triggers a <code>/capture</code> skill in Claude Code. Whatever I am reading gets captured with metadata, tagged by project, and added to a searchable journal.</p>

          <p><strong>A tag taxonomy that ties findings back to specific work.</strong> When I am making a decision on the AI Entrepreneurship Portfolio, I can pull captures tagged with it. When I am preparing a workshop, I pull training-related captures. The tagging is the glue between reading and doing.</p>

          <p><strong>A practice of integration over accumulation.</strong> I write up what each capture changes about how I think. The writing is what makes the work stick, and the public version of that writing lives in the <a href="/notes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Notes</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="mt-8 mb-4 opacity-75 text-sm italic">
        Details kept high-level to protect proprietary information. Full artifacts available upon request.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    html {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
    }
  }

  h2[id], h3[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .practice-summary {
    list-style: none;
  }
  .practice-summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .toc-link.is-active {
    opacity: 1;
    border-left-color: hsl(var(--bc));
  }

  .toc-link:focus-visible,
  a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid hsl(var(--bc));
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .not-prose p {
    color: rgb(55 65 81);
  }
</style>

<script>
  function setupScrollSpy() {
    const tocLinks = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.toc-link');
    if (tocLinks.length === 0) return;

    const sections = Array.from(tocLinks)
      .map((link) => {
        const href = link.getAttribute('href') || '';
        const id = href.startsWith('#') ? href.slice(1) : '';
        return { link, section: document.getElementById(id) };
      })
      .filter((item): item is { link: HTMLAnchorElement; section: HTMLElement } => item.section !== null);

    if (sections.length === 0) return;

    const visibleSections = new Set<HTMLElement>();

    const updateActive = () => {
      if (visibleSections.size === 0) return;
      let topSection: HTMLElement | null = null;
      let topY = Infinity;
      visibleSections.forEach((section) => {
        const rect = section.getBoundingClientRect();
        if (rect.top < topY) {
          topY = rect.top;
          topSection = section;
        }
      });
      sections.forEach((item) => {
        item.link.classList.toggle('is-active', item.section === topSection);
      });
    };

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            visibleSections.add(entry.target as HTMLElement);
          } else {
            visibleSections.delete(entry.target as HTMLElement);
          }
        });
        updateActive();
      },
      {
        rootMargin: '-10% 0px -60% 0px',
        threshold: 0,
      }
    );

    sections.forEach((item) => observer.observe(item.section));

    sections.forEach((item) => {
      item.link.addEventListener('click', () => {
        sections.forEach((s) => s.link.classList.toggle('is-active', s === item));
      });
    });
  }

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', setupScrollSpy);
</script>
